WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.603
Did you know that camels

00:00:01.603 --> 00:00:04.732
used to live in the Arctic tundra?

00:00:04.732 --> 00:00:09.310
Yes, camels! Walking
around on ice and snow.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.982
It's true. In 2013, scientists announced

00:00:12.982 --> 00:00:16.988
they'd discovered mummified
leg bones on Ellesmere Island,

00:00:16.988 --> 00:00:20.251
which belonged to the
ancestors of modern-day

00:00:20.251 --> 00:00:22.479
one- and two-humped camels.

00:00:22.479 --> 00:00:25.226
In fact, enduring the frigid tundra

00:00:25.226 --> 00:00:26.949
is how scientists think camels

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:29.900
got their iconic hump in the first place,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.539
because what's inside helped them survive

00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:36.491
in an age when many other
animals were wiped out.

00:00:36.491 --> 00:00:38.784
John Hare: What's inside
a camel's hump is fat,

00:00:38.784 --> 00:00:41.000
and a lot of people think it's water.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.900
But it's certainly not; it's fat,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.770
and it nourishes them when
they're on long journeys.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.494
Narrator: That's right, fat.

00:00:48.494 --> 00:00:52.038
Each hump can store up
to 36 kilograms of it,

00:00:52.038 --> 00:00:53.993
which can sustain the camel for weeks

00:00:53.993 --> 00:00:56.492
or even months without food.

00:00:56.492 --> 00:00:59.168
And that sort of adaptation
was especially important

00:00:59.168 --> 00:01:03.904
3.5 million years ago, during
the middle of an ice age,

00:01:03.904 --> 00:01:06.036
when the ancestors to modern camels

00:01:06.036 --> 00:01:09.125
were hanging out in the Arctic tundra.

00:01:09.125 --> 00:01:10.618
Hare: Talking about the Ice Age, when

00:01:10.618 --> 00:01:13.240
a lot of mammals were
killed during that time,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.883
and yet the camel managed to survive

00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:19.716
by developing this emergency
food system, if you like:

00:01:19.716 --> 00:01:20.849
the hump.

00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:23.170
Narrator: Eventually,
camels migrated across

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.241
the Bering Strait into
regions of Asia and Africa,

00:01:27.241 --> 00:01:28.610
where the fat inside their humps

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.988
helped them adapt yet again.

00:01:30.988 --> 00:01:33.490
This time, to the
blistering-hot temperatures

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.251
of deserts like the Gobi and Sahara.

00:01:36.251 --> 00:01:38.903
You see, camels are
one of the only animals

00:01:38.903 --> 00:01:42.678
in the world that store
all their fat in one spot.

00:01:42.678 --> 00:01:45.731
And that's useful for
keeping cool in a hot climate

00:01:45.731 --> 00:01:49.230
because heat can escape faster
from the rest of their body,

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.713
which helps them maintain
a lower body temperature.

00:01:52.713 --> 00:01:55.073
Compare that to other
mammals, like humans,

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:57.364
who store fat all over,

00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:00.255
making it a lot harder to stay cool.

00:02:00.255 --> 00:02:02.753
Today, camels still use
the fat in their humps

00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:06.495
as a food reserve, but
they're not the only ones.

00:02:06.495 --> 00:02:10.010
In extreme circumstances,
the Turkana tribe in Kenya,

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.280
for example, will eat
camel fat to survive.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.490
Hare: They suffer a lot from periods

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.992
of extreme drought, and
I have seen these people,

00:02:19.992 --> 00:02:22.453
they've been very, very short on food,

00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:25.248
and this is difficult to
believe, but it's true,

00:02:25.248 --> 00:02:28.231
slit open the top of a camel's hump,

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:31.244
take out the fat for
their own consumption,

00:02:31.244 --> 00:02:33.992
and then put the top of
the hump back on again.

00:02:33.992 --> 00:02:35.992
Narrator: But don't worry, the camel makes

00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:39.231
a full recovery, and instances are rare.

00:02:39.231 --> 00:02:41.982
But this practice has
started to generate some buzz

00:02:41.982 --> 00:02:45.483
around camel fat as a new superfood.

00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:48.736
Turns out, camel fat is
loaded with fatty acids,

00:02:48.736 --> 00:02:50.697
vitamins, and minerals.

00:02:50.697 --> 00:02:54.001
Desert Farms, a company
that sells camel fat,

00:02:54.001 --> 00:02:56.955
says that just 1 tablespoon contains

00:02:56.955 --> 00:03:00.670
40% of your daily vitamin B12 needs

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.221
and three times the amount of oleic acid

00:03:03.221 --> 00:03:06.177
than coconut oil, a superfood staple.

00:03:06.177 --> 00:03:07.990
And since all that nutritious fat

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:09.747
is what fills the hump out,

00:03:09.747 --> 00:03:12.448
when a camel fasts for long periods,

00:03:12.448 --> 00:03:14.417
its hump can actually go limp.

00:03:14.417 --> 00:03:17.145
Hare: The humps gradually
diminish in size.

00:03:17.145 --> 00:03:19.330
If it's been in a very harsh environment,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.478
they go completely limp
and flop over the side

00:03:22.478 --> 00:03:24.590
of the camel's backbone.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.029
Narrator: Now, since we know that fat

00:03:26.029 --> 00:03:28.497
is what makes up the
hump instead of water,

00:03:28.497 --> 00:03:30.142
that got us wondering:

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:33.742
How do camels stay hydrated
in such dry climates?

00:03:33.742 --> 00:03:36.194
Well, they have unique blood cells

00:03:36.194 --> 00:03:38.487
that run throughout their entire body,

00:03:38.487 --> 00:03:41.180
including a few in the hump itself.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.023
These blood cells are extremely elastic,

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.920
perfect for holding a lot of water.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.747
Camels can drink up to 115
liters in about 10 minutes,

00:03:51.747 --> 00:03:56.024
expanding the cells up
to 240% in the process.

00:03:56.024 --> 00:03:58.649
Hare: There are capillaries
throughout its body,

00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:02.265
and when it has a drink, it
drinks and drinks and drinks,

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:06.017
and it swells up, and it
looks as though it's pregnant.

00:04:06.017 --> 00:04:07.844
Narrator: If that's not impressive enough,

00:04:07.844 --> 00:04:10.751
the wild camel of China
has even been known

00:04:10.751 --> 00:04:13.260
to survive on salt water.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.011
So, whether it's surviving
the harsh desert heat,

00:04:17.011 --> 00:04:19.526
weeks without food and water,

00:04:19.526 --> 00:04:21.495
or even the Canadian Arctic,

00:04:21.495 --> 00:04:23.594
the camel is one of the best adapters

00:04:23.594 --> 00:04:26.744
in the animal kingdom,
and that's in large part

00:04:26.744 --> 00:04:29.043
thanks to its iconic hump.

